# Copy to `.env.local` to run the site against a local Supabase stack (`npm run db:start`).
#
# The two `NEXT_PUBLIC_` values are public by design: they ship in the browser bundle, and row level
# security is what protects the data. Take them from `supabase status` (API URL and anon key).
#
# `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` is the opposite: it bypasses RLS, so it is server-only and must never be
# prefixed `NEXT_PUBLIC_` or read in a client component. `POST /api/progress` needs it to write
# `attempts` and `lesson_progress`, which learners hold read-only grants on. Leave it unset and the
# route answers 503 `progress_write_unconfigured` instead of falling back to the anon key.
#
# Unset is a supported state for all three: the auth pages render an explanation, the proxy returns
# early and the progress route refuses the write, so the drills work exactly as before.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54351
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ── Payments (phase 4, item 6) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `npm run stripe:seed` creates the one product and its three prices in whatever account
# `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY` belongs to, and prints the three `STRIPE_PRICE_*` lines to paste below. It is
# idempotent by `lookup_key`, so running it twice does not create a second product.
#
# Unset is a supported state, and it is the state CI is in: the pricing page says checkout is closed,
# the three routes answer 503, and nothing else in the site changes. When `STRIPE_SECRET_KEY` is set at
# build time the pricing page swaps that note for a plain form post to `/api/stripe/checkout?plan=annual`
# — a form, not a script, so the offer still works without JavaScript. Both builds read the same
# variables, which is what keeps the static page and the Next page identical.
#
# `STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET` is the only thing that lets a purchase be believed: with it unset
# `POST /api/stripe/webhook` refuses every delivery with 503, whatever it contains. Locally:
#
#   stripe listen --forward-to 127.0.0.1:4174/api/stripe/webhook
#
# which prints the `whsec_…` to paste here. Test mode only, and never a live key in a local file.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
STRIPE_PRICE_MONTHLY=
STRIPE_PRICE_ANNUAL=
STRIPE_PRICE_LIFETIME=

# ── Retention kill switches (phase 5, item 7) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Each one turns its own retention surface off on a running deployment without a rebuild, so a defect in
# one is a degraded feature instead of a launch blocker. Read at request time on the server only — never
# prefixed `NEXT_PUBLIC_`, so the browser cannot see a flag and cannot disagree with the server about it.
#
# Default is ON, and only `off`, `0`, `false` or `no` (any case) turns one off; a missing variable, an
# empty one, or anything unrecognised leaves the feature running, because a typo that silently hides a
# working feature is worse than one that changes nothing. Unset is the CI state and changes nothing.
#
#   RETENTION_STREAKS=off          hides the streak rows on /account and the board on /leaderboard, and
#                                  stops the write moving a streak — points are still paid.
#   RETENTION_DAILY_CHALLENGE=off  /daily and /daily/run explain themselves; POST /api/daily answers 404.
#   RETENTION_FLASHCARDS=off       /flashcards explains itself; GET /api/flashcards answers 404.
#
# With accounts unconfigured the four pages are static, so their flag branch is baked at build time. With
# accounts configured they read the environment per request; that is the case these switches are for.
RETENTION_STREAKS=
RETENTION_DAILY_CHALLENGE=
RETENTION_FLASHCARDS=
